    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain email blocklist by following the codebase's established pattern for owner-creation business rules (a small single-responsibility `@Component` validator that throws a dedicated exception, mapped to an HTTP status by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`).\n\n**New files:**\n- `rest/validation/DisposableEmailDomainValidator.java` \u2014 extracts the email's domain (case-insensitively) and throws when it's one of `mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`. A null email is accepted, matching the \"email is optional\" contract.\n- `rest/validation/DisposableEmailDomainException.java` \u2014 carries the rejected value for logging and exposes `FIELD = \"email\"`, mirroring the sibling exceptions.\n\n**Edited files:**\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` \u2014 injected the validator and call it right after `emailNormalizer.normalize(...)`, so the domain check runs against the already-normalized (lower-cased, syntactically valid) email.\n- `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2014 added a handler returning **400 Bad Request** with `ValidationErrorsResponse(List.of(...FIELD))`, identical in shape to the existing `InvalidEmailException`/`InvalidTelephoneException` handlers.\n\nI deliberately kept the blocklist policy out of `EmailNormalizer` (whose sole job is normalization/syntactic validation) rather than letting it become a catch-all. The acceptance test `errorRejectsDisposableDomain` passes, and the full suite is green.",
